                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Kids Return\"] on certain days i think this melancholy portrait of young blindly navigating the passage into adulthood is kitano\u0027s finest work - a delicate synthesis of the editing and mise en scene experiments of his previous films, applied to arguably the most layered character work of his career (only achilles and the tortoise comes close for me). boxing becomes not a central metaphor but the physical shape of the film\u0027s emotional weights - every punch struck (in or out of the ring), every physical pleasure indulged in or denied, the journey from youthful \"prank\" violence to its more permanent twin. and i",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/kids-return/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Kids Return\"] life.a neverending battle between who i am and who i hope to one day be. feeling lost among a sea of people. do you see what i see?do you like who you are?do you, too, question why happiness seems so far?...my tears are flowing.life.a roundabout where each lane leads to some unavoidable pain.painted portraits of hurtful histories in every nook of my brain.do your struggles look like mine?do your mistakes sink their fangs into your psyche?do you, too, often wonder \"are they just like me?\"...i am tired of not knowing.life.where darkness can never fully stop beauty from blossoming.we\u0027re still her",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/kids-return/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Kids Return\"] I don\u0027t think I could\u0027ve watched this film at a more pivotal moment in my life. It\u0027s also a film that\u0027s evaded me the longest out of Kitano\u0027s body of work. For that reason alone it feels almost past what you\u0027d call coincidental, as if it\u0027s a direct message. One that truly affected me on a more personal note than ever before in the context of a Takeshi Kitano film. Man, It was tough feeling the desparity when I found the pressure, the hopelessness and the lack of purpose a highly relatable feeling in this film. It\u0027s also his first film after his near fatal motor accident and it\u0027s quite telling",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/kids-return/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Kids Return\"] Another great movie from Takeshi Kitano, a movie about childhood, nostalgia, and inevitable failure. This was so relatable that I just wanted to cry the whole time, this movie questioned myself about my future. Joe Hisaichi\u0027s soundtrack is always here to hit you deeply, and it did it again in the movie. The ending is amazing, and contains a good message that even if you fail, you must stay who you are! And never give up! Amazing Movie 🤍!Masaru, do you think we\u0027re already finished ?Hell no. We haven\u0027t even started.",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/kids-return/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Kids Return\"] Films about disaffected youth are not exactly a rarity; it is how they are executed that makes all the difference. What Takeshi Kitano does here is nothing short of an achievement because not only does he make the kind of film which has been done many times before, he manages to make it fresh and new.Kids Return is heartfelt, resembling life closer than most films of its kind ever manage to do. My emotional investment in these characters\u0027 lives were due in large part to Joe Hisaishi\u0027s sublime score. Each second of each scene is elevated from an emotional standpoint because of his tender music.",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/kids-return/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Wikipedia: Kids Return",
                                   "snippet":  "[Wikipedia Synopsis] Kids Return (キッズ・リターン, Kizzu Ritān) is a 1996 Japanese film written, edited and directed by Takeshi Kitano. The film was made directly after Kitano recovered from a motorcycle wreck that left one side of his body paralyzed. After undergoing extensive surgery and physical therapy, he quickly went about making Kids Return amidst speculation that he might never be able to work again. The movie is about two high school dropouts, Masaru (Ken Kaneko) and Shinji (Masanobu Andō), who try to find a direction and meaning in their lives—one by becoming a yakuza lieutenant, the other by becoming a boxer.\nThe music was composed by Joe Hisaishi, and the cinematographer was Katsumi Yanagishima.",
                                   "link":  "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kids%20Return"
                               }
